#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/id #uuid "d97c3cf2-b627-5274-853a-be3d7e5cda40" :question/math-academy-id "e-10246" :question/problem "![](images/02/025ea896b8ef2253530a6392fc5b5c89c70df5083a673de14a1c6e13108068bd.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,2].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,2]$ given that $f(2) = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,2),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-2,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $1$ triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1bcd6d6800caf6abdca868bba4abff3935f26e984807f13c25d4368dc858f2c1.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =4 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(-6+8) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{4,-4,2\\} = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/id #uuid "b28b6939-847d-51ab-a97d-4367323d0d01" :question/math-academy-id "e-10247" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,6].$ The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9, 3$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,6]$ given that $f(6) = 2.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab98f2f27ff11ddb40138bf09900a865ca006bc8f26c95dfe03ea17fd4e49387.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-6,6),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =2 \\\\ f(3) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-5) \\\\  & =7 \\\\ f(0) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(3-5) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-6) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{-6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{-6}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-9+3-5) \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{2, 7, 4, 13\\} = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{min} = 2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188026 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b22c6bd-c098-53af-bac1-500de0b75ed2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/id #uuid "71cc1f65-437f-5928-82c2-be3dc4b0985f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10248" :question/problem "![](images/69/69d2d66022521424f9c6cda6ad901eb9ed1dbdb01044f5fa2d2954049770a3d4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,4].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9,$ $7$ and $8,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,4]$ given that $f(1) = 10.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-4,4),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,1,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =10 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10-(-7) \\\\  & =10+7 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10-(-9-7) \\\\  & =10-(-16) \\\\  & =10+16 \\\\  & =26 \\\\ f(4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10+8 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{10,17,26,18\\} = 26.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{max} = 26,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is  $x_\\text{max} = -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188027 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f2efc16-254b-5723-937e-60ad37132c98" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/id #uuid "708db737-4215-5df0-8940-c9d17dbd2b5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10249" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,5].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2,$ $14$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,5]$ given that $f(3) = -2.$\n\n![](images/de/deb5d80fe70d31fe768a180f3afd8a6054eda8e9fc3d1d0cd52c696c9de47660.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-5,5),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-3$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,-3,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-2) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-14 \\\\  & =-16 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-2+14) \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{-2, -4, -16, -14\\} = -16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{min} = -16,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = -3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b47647-2bba-505f-8d7e-311d4a4c980d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185425} {:answer/id #uuid "c30e5ea3-e2d6-5c94-88aa-d763c1948734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185426} {:answer/id #uuid "c68099e9-f773-54d9-a3e5-42bd9bdb5311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185427} {:answer/id #uuid "d288b09f-39a9-530c-aa67-bbf726fea5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185428} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bca3e6-7175-5376-9f18-97b44e4a39fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d288b09f-39a9-530c-aa67-bbf726fea5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185428} :answer-field/id #uuid "b38f958f-f702-57d9-a4a7-e10129a07961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee6627c5-4c31-5514-890e-94be51b161ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-104045" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f757ffa45b20e043ad461cb0dfa9c486cf73466b1d8d87fa0ffad00bc01af960.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $1,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5]$ given that $f(5)=-8.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =-8 \\\\ f(3) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{3}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8-1 \\\\  & =-9 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8-(-4+1) \\\\  & =-8-(-3) \\\\  & =-8+3 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-8,-9,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141d92b1-19f8-549f-9528-6eca52259abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519026} {:answer/id #uuid "45425c6b-a98b-51fa-8eca-55d7ba992558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519099} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9c6ce2-7d9b-519d-97b9-3134db09d6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519160} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f87cad-f947-5cf2-a0e5-dd1577365c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519283} {:answer/id #uuid "c32c0b40-a807-5d90-aa7a-5b8fcac8a181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f9c6ce2-7d9b-519d-97b9-3134db09d6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519160} :answer-field/id #uuid "a91548fc-7110-5f63-9d3c-0095f5b5ffe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1395e862-a7e8-537a-97de-69c4caaca685" :question/math-academy-id "q-144839" :question/problem "![](images/21/219e476902934a4272fb0c523fe0b623f27ef5ba2647fcf4658d0b262e64200e.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(5)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =3 \\\\ f(0) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-8 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{3,-5\\}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9984ad-b58e-50e4-9a28-2c6aa4654635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e9984ad-b58e-50e4-9a28-2c6aa4654635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519014} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad41d34f-e05e-58cf-bab8-f5b61c5398a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13ad1074-2be7-58b6-8b52-de567a736fd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-316301" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcffe721c35a3c729644fb36ee9008095c46298a56109f0dfd75a3c06f3f7cae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right).\n\nGiven that $f(2)=-3,$ what is the maximum value ${f}_{\\text{max}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(-2) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =-3-(4+(-2)) \\\\  & =-3-2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3,-1,-5\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87b49dea-876c-5d58-b701-71dbe94190f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87b49dea-876c-5d58-b701-71dbe94190f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519208} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7bf91a3-f797-5574-a5b8-6124d6526096" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebf67b7c-064d-5c23-8f05-31612119932c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344100" :question/problem "![](images/95/955cda48e8e98f46903a471ceefa657a6032cb2e6feab304569a62750eba9825.png)\n\nThe function $r(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,7].$ The graph of ${r}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $r(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$\n\nGiven that $r(7)=6,$ what is the maximum value ${r}_{\\text{max}}$ of $r(x)$ on the interval $[-1,7]?$\n\n${r}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $r(x)$ on $[-1,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${r}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,7),$ and ${r}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $r$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(7) & =6 \\\\ r(-1) & =r(7)+{\\int }_{7}^{-1}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =r(7)-{\\int }_{-1}^{7}{r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6-(-10) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $r$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $r$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{6,16\\}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $r$ is ${r}_{\\text{max}}=16,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $r(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b8d084-32d1-5d13-b2b6-29a387928867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07b8d084-32d1-5d13-b2b6-29a387928867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519002} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0754120-f98a-50c1-b1fd-4396f034bf3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b09a5298-e405-5905-9c2a-958d82f56eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344104" :question/problem "![](images/21/21e0ed364f3a04068c7f3775715432b218be2ada6b54d97ae6836f28528006da.png)\n\nThe function $s(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${s}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $s(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$\n\nGiven that $s(9)=-2,$ what is the maximum value ${s}_{\\text{max}}$ of $s(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]?$\n\n${s}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $s(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${s}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${s}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $s$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s(9) & =-2 \\\\ s(0) & =s(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{0}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =s(9)-{\\int }_{0}^{9}{s}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2-(-6) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $s$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $s$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{s}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,4\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $s$ is ${s}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $s(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3641a945-2d7f-53df-9532-8edc301d039a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3641a945-2d7f-53df-9532-8edc301d039a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519075} :answer-field/id #uuid "139d21af-01a8-549d-89ac-a4e1d1e8fbed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a322e8e4-fc5c-530f-aece-0d3557ac2a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-344108" :question/problem "![](images/84/84deebb7aa20aee2bb0b9e57db825fbdfac1482ca4aec7202ba5458c5603492f.png)\n\nThe function $t(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${t}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $t(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$\n\nGiven that $t(2)=3,$ what is the maximum value ${t}_{\\text{max}}$ of $t(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]?$\n\n${t}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $t(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${t}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${t}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $t$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(2) & =3 \\\\ t(-4) & =t(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-4}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =t(2)-{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{t}^{′}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3-(-8) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $t$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $t$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{t}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $t$ is ${t}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $t(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe41923-7be4-516e-afbe-7eb6f9a11be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fe41923-7be4-516e-afbe-7eb6f9a11be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519229} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfb1f5aa-b5e4-53c8-93c1-41b509c0b3ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82901cf9-17f2-500f-9676-c95d18dab327" :question/math-academy-id "q-344166" :question/problem "![](images/31/31fd7b63abbf8224421ee53b0703e9cc72fac2f9a8ffc3e460218bb42c4f229a.png)\n\nThe function $q(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,9].$ The graph of ${q}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $q(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $12.$\n\nGiven that $q(9)=-1,$ what is the minimum value ${q}_{\\text{min}}$ of $q(x)$ on the interval $[1,9]?$\n\n${q}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $q(x)$ on $[1,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${q}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,9),$ and ${q}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $q$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(9) & =-1 \\\\ q(1) & =q(9)+{\\int }_{9}^{1}{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =q(9)-{\\int }_{1}^{9}{q}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1-12 \\\\  & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $q$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $q$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{q}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,-13\\}=-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $q$ is ${q}_{\\text{min}}=-13,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $q(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ff0eb8e-cde3-5239-b043-47a136211ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ff0eb8e-cde3-5239-b043-47a136211ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519161} :answer-field/id #uuid "c40dea29-2b27-54dd-9da2-418a3d1e8a16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e747dd9d-512a-59fa-9944-1137ebe8b2f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344167" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dd61819859d65af85ef462c7fb694ecbfd2640aa62f41ff0e27e5bb8073f48c.png)\n\nThe function $a(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${a}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $a(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $11.$\n\nGiven that $a(5)=-4,$ what is the minimum value ${a}_{\\text{min}}$ of $a(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]?$\n\n${a}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $a(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${a}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${a}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $a$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(5) & =-4 \\\\ a(-2) & =a(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =a(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{a}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4-(-11) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $a$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $a$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{7,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $a$ is ${a}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $a(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee602556-4b14-51e0-a262-9ca407ca2dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee602556-4b14-51e0-a262-9ca407ca2dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519383} :answer-field/id #uuid "371f6b9e-7664-5f83-883e-450ac8a167d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3fba9ae-fcbd-5c75-9ad3-403a4a333328" :question/math-academy-id "q-344182" :question/problem "![](images/04/04e4299da4d4c29cce751d04ee6ee29380a3dead3ab3456f6645858b5d9d106b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(3)=2,$ what is the minimum value ${f}_{\\text{min}}$ of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,3]?$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/c3/c30d7e1d2c9eb939726d85b0ee5d51a2bdc0cfe73fab92f0ba29839a9f18be4e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-7.5 \\\\  & =-5.5 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-3+7.5) \\\\  & =2-4.5 \\\\  & =-2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{2,-5.5,-2.5\\}=-5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$"}]}
